			"index": "b1M",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 697197100,
			"version": 491,
			"versionNonce": 2081427348,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783415151504,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "- enqueue(value) Operation: Push incoming data directly onto stack1.\n    Time Complexity: O(1) constant time. The data is simply placed on top of the \n    collection.\n\n- dequeue() Operation: Check if stack2 contains elements. \n  If it does, execute a standard pop() from stack2. \n  If stack2 is empty, iteratively pop every element out of stack1 and immediately push it \n    into stack2 until stack1 is completely exhausted. Once this structural transfer is\n    complete, perform the final pop() from stack2.\n  Time Complexity: Amortized O(1) (Worst-Case O(n)). \n    Although a transfer routine is triggered whenever stack2 goes empty—resulting in a \n    single linear execution cost of O(n)—each individual item is only moved across the \n    stack boundary exactly twice. \n    Over a long sequence of operations, this distributes the cost evenly, maintaining a\n    constant amortized average cost of O(1) per operation.",
			"rawText": "- enqueue(value) Operation: Push incoming data directly onto stack1.\n    Time Complexity: O(1) constant time. The data is simply placed on top of the \n    collection.\n\n- dequeue() Operation: Check if stack2 contains elements. \n  If it does, execute a standard pop() from stack2. \n  If stack2 is empty, iteratively pop every element out of stack1 and immediately push it \n    into stack2 until stack1 is completely exhausted. Once this structural transfer is\n    complete, perform the final pop() from stack2.\n  Time Complexity: Amortized O(1) (Worst-Case O(n)). \n    Although a transfer routine is triggered whenever stack2 goes empty—resulting in a \n    single linear execution cost of O(n)—each individual item is only moved across the \n    stack boundary exactly twice. \n    Over a long sequence of operations, this distributes the cost evenly, maintaining a\n    constant amortized average cost of O(1) per operation.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "- enqueue(value) Operation: Push incoming data directly onto stack1.\n    Time Complexity: O(1) constant time. The data is simply placed on top of the \n    collection.\n\n- dequeue() Operation: Check if stack2 contains elements. \n  If it does, execute a standard pop() from stack2. \n  If stack2 is empty, iteratively pop every element out of stack1 and immediately push it \n    into stack2 until stack1 is completely exhausted. Once this structural transfer is\n    complete, perform the final pop() from stack2.\n  Time Complexity: Amortized O(1) (Worst-Case O(n)). \n    Although a transfer routine is triggered whenever stack2 goes empty—resulting in a \n    single linear execution cost of O(n)—each individual item is only moved across the \n    stack boundary exactly twice. \n    Over a long sequence of operations, this distributes the cost evenly, maintaining a\n    constant amortized average cost of O(1) per operation.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
