		{
			"id": "R71BqfP7",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 3066.298041148866,
			"y": -504.8472673695343,
			"width": 1374.9984130859375,
			"height": 725,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b2W",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 232358420,
			"version": 172,
			"versionNonce": 1743044396,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1783417163745,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "1. The Deque (Double-Ended Queue)A Deque (pronounced \"deck\", short for Double-Ended Queue) is a linear sequence that\ngeneralizes both Stacks and Queues. \nInstead of restricting operations to one or two specific ends, a Deque allows you to insert and delete elements from both the\nfront and the back with equal permission.\n    Why it matters: It is the ultimate hybrid.\n    If you restrict a Deque to only use insertFront and deleteFront, it behaves exactly like a Stack.\n    If you restrict a Deque to only use insertBack and deleteFront, it behaves exactly like a Queue.\n        The Exam Trap: If a question asks you to implement a Deque with $O(1)$ runtime for all four operations using a Linked\n        List, a Singly Linked List will fail. You must use a Doubly Linked List because deleting from the back (deleteBack) \n        requires immediate access to the tail's predecessor node.\n2. Priority Queues (The Concept)You will see this term pop up frequently. \nA Priority Queue is an abstract data type that looks like a queue, but with a structural twist: every element inserted has an\nassociated priority rating (usually a number).The Rule: Elements are not removed based on arrival time (FIFO). \nInstead, the element with the highest priority is dequeued first. If elements have the same priority, they fallback to\nstandard FIFO order.\n    Why it matters for your syllabus: While a priority queue can be built using a sorted linked list, doing so forces an \n    expensive O(n) insertion time. \n\n    To make it highly efficient, computer scientists implement Priority Queues using a specialized binary tree structure \n    called a Heap (which gives you $O(\\log n)$ performance).\n\n3. Strict Structural Vocabulary: Overflow vs. UnderflowExaminers look for these precise terms when grading debugging or edge-\ncase handling code blocks:Queue/Stack Underflow: This occurs when your code attempts to pop or dequeue from a structure that\nis completely empty (size == 0 or top == null). If you don't check for this using an isEmpty() guard condition, your program\nwill crash with a NullPointerException or IllegalStateException.Queue/Stack Overflow: This occurs when your code attempts to\npush or enqueue an item into a structure that has reached its maximum storage capacity.Linked List implementations\npractically never experience overflow because they allocate heap memory dynamically until the computer runs out of RAM.Array-\nbased implementations (including Circular Queues) always have a fixed capacity and will strike an overflow condition the\nmoment size == capacity.",
			"rawText": "1. The Deque (Double-Ended Queue)A Deque (pronounced \"deck\", short for Double-Ended Queue) is a linear sequence that generalizes both Stacks and Queues. \nInstead of restricting operations to one or two specific ends, a Deque allows you to insert and delete elements from both the front and the back with equal permission.\n    Why it matters: It is the ultimate hybrid.\n    If you restrict a Deque to only use insertFront and deleteFront, it behaves exactly like a Stack.\n    If you restrict a Deque to only use insertBack and deleteFront, it behaves exactly like a Queue.\n        The Exam Trap: If a question asks you to implement a Deque with $O(1)$ runtime for all four operations using a Linked \n        List, a Singly Linked List will fail. You must use a Doubly Linked List because deleting from the back (deleteBack) \n        requires immediate access to the tail's predecessor node.\n2. Priority Queues (The Concept)You will see this term pop up frequently. \nA Priority Queue is an abstract data type that looks like a queue, but with a structural twist: every element inserted has an associated priority rating (usually a number).The Rule: Elements are not removed based on arrival time (FIFO). \nInstead, the element with the highest priority is dequeued first. If elements have the same priority, they fallback to standard FIFO order.\n    Why it matters for your syllabus: While a priority queue can be built using a sorted linked list, doing so forces an \n    expensive O(n) insertion time. \n\n    To make it highly efficient, computer scientists implement Priority Queues using a specialized binary tree structure \n    called a Heap (which gives you $O(\\log n)$ performance).\n\n3. Strict Structural Vocabulary: Overflow vs. UnderflowExaminers look for these precise terms when grading debugging or edge-case handling code blocks:Queue/Stack Underflow: This occurs when your code attempts to pop or dequeue from a structure that is completely empty (size == 0 or top == null). If you don't check for this using an isEmpty() guard condition, your program will crash with a NullPointerException or IllegalStateException.Queue/Stack Overflow: This occurs when your code attempts to push or enqueue an item into a structure that has reached its maximum storage capacity.Linked List implementations practically never experience overflow because they allocate heap memory dynamically until the computer runs out of RAM.Array-based implementations (including Circular Queues) always have a fixed capacity and will strike an overflow condition the moment size == capacity.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": "oIJWZDKb",
			"originalText": "1. The Deque (Double-Ended Queue)A Deque (pronounced \"deck\", short for Double-Ended Queue) is a linear sequence that generalizes both Stacks and Queues. \nInstead of restricting operations to one or two specific ends, a Deque allows you to insert and delete elements from both the front and the back with equal permission.\n    Why it matters: It is the ultimate hybrid.\n    If you restrict a Deque to only use insertFront and deleteFront, it behaves exactly like a Stack.\n    If you restrict a Deque to only use insertBack and deleteFront, it behaves exactly like a Queue.\n        The Exam Trap: If a question asks you to implement a Deque with $O(1)$ runtime for all four operations using a Linked \n        List, a Singly Linked List will fail. You must use a Doubly Linked List because deleting from the back (deleteBack) \n        requires immediate access to the tail's predecessor node.\n2. Priority Queues (The Concept)You will see this term pop up frequently. \nA Priority Queue is an abstract data type that looks like a queue, but with a structural twist: every element inserted has an associated priority rating (usually a number).The Rule: Elements are not removed based on arrival time (FIFO). \nInstead, the element with the highest priority is dequeued first. If elements have the same priority, they fallback to standard FIFO order.\n    Why it matters for your syllabus: While a priority queue can be built using a sorted linked list, doing so forces an \n    expensive O(n) insertion time. \n\n    To make it highly efficient, computer scientists implement Priority Queues using a specialized binary tree structure \n    called a Heap (which gives you $O(\\log n)$ performance).\n\n3. Strict Structural Vocabulary: Overflow vs. UnderflowExaminers look for these precise terms when grading debugging or edge-case handling code blocks:Queue/Stack Underflow: This occurs when your code attempts to pop or dequeue from a structure that is completely empty (size == 0 or top == null). If you don't check for this using an isEmpty() guard condition, your program will crash with a NullPointerException or IllegalStateException.Queue/Stack Overflow: This occurs when your code attempts to push or enqueue an item into a structure that has reached its maximum storage capacity.Linked List implementations practically never experience overflow because they allocate heap memory dynamically until the computer runs out of RAM.Array-based implementations (including Circular Queues) always have a fixed capacity and will strike an overflow condition the moment size == capacity.",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
